              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for a cousin, then the Latin for twin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: noverca, novercae",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "noverca, novercae — \"a stepmother\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Noverca fīliōs marītī amat. The stepmother loves her husband's sons."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine, first declension. In Roman stories the stepmother is often cruel, but not always."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "noverca is usually traced to novus \"new\" (Lewis & Short; EtymDB): the new wife of the house."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
